      "text": "To find the median of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we first need to determine the value of \\(n\\). The median of a set of five numbers is the third number when the numbers are arranged in ascending order. The set is already in ascending order, so the median is the third number, which is \\(n + 6\\). We are given that the median is 9. Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\nn + 6 = 9\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\), we subtract 6 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nn = 3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of \\(n\\), we can substitute it back into the set to get the actual numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the mean of the set \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\). The mean is the sum of the numbers divided by the number of elements in the set. The sum of the numbers is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 40\n\\]\n\nThere are 5 numbers in the set, so the mean is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{40}{5} = 8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
